# Reviewed credential-scan baseline.
#
# Each line is a fingerprint (path + truncated salted hash), never a value.
# Adding a line means a human decided the finding is not an unrotated live
# credential. Every entry needs a comment saying why, and a way out.
#
# Format: <path>:<hash>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KNOWN EXPOSURE — NOT ACCEPTED, ONLY RECORDED. See issue #12.
#
# Portal request headers for the Meri Panchayat / Panchayat Nirnay APIs,
# merged to dev in b5c2edc and e69daf2 and live in a public repository. They
# are baselined solely so this scanner can be turned on now and block *new*
# leaks instead of waiting for the cleanup.
#
# Per #12 these are the portals' own client-side request values, not
# credentials issued to this project, so there is nothing for us to revoke.
# `secretkey` is bound to `timestamp` and both entries below are months
# stale, so neither is usable. The exposure that does matter is the
# operational identifiers alongside them -- `device-ip` and `uuid` pin one
# operator's address and session into a public repo. #28 removes all of it
# from tracked source, after which this whole section goes away.
#
# Do not add anything else to this section.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
src/ingest/Panchayat_Nirnay/config.py:b69d5840f00082a0  # portal access key
src/ingest/Panchayat_Nirnay/config.py:27a86a85d36fbf3b  # portal secret key, dev tip
# A third value: the file tells the next operator to paste in a fresh
# secretkey when the scraper halts, so each refresh commits a new one. This
# one is only reachable through history (e69daf2), not from any branch tip,
# and only a range spanning that commit -- such as dev -> main -- sees it.
src/ingest/Panchayat_Nirnay/config.py:655ebdfe4f800f64  # portal secret key, e69daf2
